Answer:
Animals employ diverse reproductive strategies, broadly categorized into sexual and asexual reproduction, each with unique mechanisms and evolutionary benefits. Sexual reproduction, the most common method, involves the fusion of male (sperm) and female (egg) gametes, producing genetically diverse offspring. This includes internal fertilization (mammals, birds, reptiles) where sperm meets egg inside the body, and external fertilization (fish, amphibians) where eggs are fertilized in water. Some species, like earthworms or snails, are hermaphrodites, possessing both male and female organs for reciprocal mating. In contrast, asexual reproduction creates genetically identical offspring from a single parent, favoring rapid colonization. Methods include:

  Binary fission (e.g., amoebas), where a parent splits into two equal cells;

  Budding (e.g., hydras), where a new individual grows from the parents body;

  Fragmentation (e.g., starfish), where severed body parts regenerate into new organisms;

  Parthenogenesis (e.g., Komodo dragons, aphids), where unfertilized eggs develop into offspring.
  Some animals, like corals or bees, exhibit alternation of generations, combining both reproductive modes. Environmental factors, such as mate availability or habitat stability, often dictate the strategy used. While sexual reproduction enhances genetic diversity and adaptability, asexual reproduction ensures efficiency and speed, showcasing natures versatility in sustaining life.
